## Summary
The article discusses Ada Lovelace, daughter of Lord Byron, who wrote the first computer program in 1842, nearly a century before the invention of the first computer. It also explores the importance of revisionist history in correcting omissions and recognizing overlooked contributions.

## Key Points
- Ada Lovelace authored the first computer program in 1842.
- She was the daughter of the poet Lord Byron.
- Her work preceded the development of modern computers by almost a hundred years.
- The article emphasizes the need to revise historical narratives to acknowledge pioneering figures.


